  "worked_solution": "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=3.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)$ exists and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=f(3)=-3.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}\\frac{x+(f(x))^{3}}{2} & =\\frac{3+(-3)^{3}}{2}=-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
